Preload libgdal.dylib before the extensions that link it, so iOS can resolve it.

flet-libgdal ships a shared libgdal.dylib for iOS as of build 3, which is what
lets every extension in this package share one GDAL -- and therefore one driver
registry, retiring ios-driver-registry.patch. Delivery is the remaining problem:
serious_python relocates each extension module into its own *.framework bundle,
while libgdal.dylib stays a plain file in site-packages/opt/lib, and nothing on
the relocated extensions' rpath points at it. dyld then fails the @rpath lookup
at first import:

    ImportError: dlopen(...): Library not loaded: @rpath/libgdal.dylib

Loading the dylib RTLD_GLOBAL before any extension is imported sidesteps the
search: dyld binds each @rpath reference by matching an already-loaded image's
install-id. The "<name>.fwork" fallback covers a future serious_python that does
relocate it, and every failure is swallowed, because on Android and on desktop
this block must be inert rather than fatal.

Proven shape -- recipes/pyarrow (libarrow, also in opt/lib) and recipes/pymupdf
(libmupdf, alongside the package) both ship it and are on-device verified.

Retires ios-driver-registry.patch, which existed only because each extension
had its own static GDAL and therefore its own driver registry -- including
rasterio.shutil, which build 12 missed.

--- a/rasterio/__init__.py
+++ b/rasterio/__init__.py
@@ -1,4 +1,47 @@
 """Rasterio"""
+
+# mobile-forge (iOS): the extension modules below link libgdal through @rpath,
+# and flet relocates each of them into its own *.framework bundle while
+# libgdal.dylib ships as a plain file in site-packages/opt/lib. Nothing on the
+# extensions' rpath resolves that, so the first import otherwise dies with
+# "Library not loaded: @rpath/libgdal.dylib". Preload it RTLD_GLOBAL first:
+# once the image is loaded, dyld satisfies each extension's @rpath reference by
+# matching the already-loaded image's install-id. If a flet build DID relocate
+# it into a framework it leaves a "libgdal.fwork" text marker holding the
+# app-relative path, so follow that instead. No-op on Android, where DT_NEEDED
+# resolves libgdal.so by basename out of jniLibs, and on desktop, where there is
+# no opt/lib to find. Same shape as recipes/pyarrow and recipes/pymupdf.
+try:
+    import ctypes as _mf_ctypes
+    import os as _mf_os
+
+    _mf_optlib = _mf_os.path.join(
+        _mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_os.path.abspath(__file__))),
+        "opt",
+        "lib",
+    )
+    _mf_dylib = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_optlib, "libgdal.dylib")
+    if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_dylib):
+        try:
+            _mf_ctypes.CDLL(_mf_dylib, mode=_mf_ctypes.RTLD_GLOBAL)
+        except OSError:
+            pass
+    else:
+        _mf_fwork = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_optlib, "libgdal.fwork")
+        if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_fwork):
+            _mf_rel = open(_mf_fwork).read().strip()
+            _mf_dir = _mf_optlib
+            for _ in range(12):
+                _mf_dir = _mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_dir)
+                _mf_candidate = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_dir, _mf_rel)
+                if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_candidate):
+                    try:
+                        _mf_ctypes.CDLL(_mf_candidate, mode=_mf_ctypes.RTLD_GLOBAL)
+                    except OSError:
+                        pass
+                    break
+except Exception:
+    pass
 
